*   [System.IO.Compression.GZipStream](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.compression.gzipstream)

data.content:
Based on my research of the .NET 11 breaking changes, here are the **4 issues** that will affect your app:

---

### 1. GZipStream: Empty output check breaks
**[Preview 1]** `GZipStream` now *always* writes headers/footers, even when no data is written. Output will be **20 bytes** (not zero) for an empty payload.

```csharp
// ❌ This will never be true in .NET 11
if (output.Length == 0) { /* "no data" */ }

// ✅ Fix: check uncompressed input length before compressing
if (dataToCompress.Length == 0) { /* "no data" */ }
```

[Docs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/compatibility/core-libraries/11/deflatestream-gzipstream-empty-payload)

---

### 2. TarReader: Checksum validation now enforced
**[Preview 1]** `TarReader` now validates header checksums on read ([dotnet/runtime#118577](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/118577)). Your hand-crafted archives with invalid checksums will throw `InvalidDataException`.

**Fix:** Regenerate archives with correct checksums, or pre-validate/repair headers before reading.

---

### 3. ZipArchive.CreateAsync: No more lazy loading
**[Preview 1]** `CreateAsync` now **eagerly** reads the central directory. Exceptions from malformed entries surface during `CreateAsync`, not when accessing `.Entries`.

```csharp
// ❌ Exceptions now thrown here, not later
var archive = await ZipArchive.CreateAsync(stream, ZipArchiveMode.Read);

// ✅ Wrap CreateAsync in try/catch for InvalidDataException
```

---

### 4. MemoryStream: Reduced max capacity & different exception
**[Preview 1]** Max capacity reduced from `int.MaxValue` (`0x7FFFFFFF`) to `Array.MaxLength` (`0x7FFFFFC7` = 2,147,483,591). Allocations in the gap now throw **`ArgumentOutOfRangeException`** instead of `OutOfMemoryException`.

```csharp
// ❌ Catches wrong exception type in .NET 11
catch (OutOfMemoryException) { }

// ✅ Also catch the new exception
catch (ArgumentOutOfRangeException) { }
// Or better: validate against Array.MaxLength before allocating
```

---

**Summary:** All four scenarios are affected by documented .NET 11 Preview 1 breaking changes in `System.IO.Compression` and `System.IO`.
